From 0765619b9dcab40c71c245c84ccc2fa046e52ed6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: vincent <vincent@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 06:11:21 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] routing: the routing history's days are the reader's, not the
 server's (#3324, yetone)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The history's files are named by this computer's day (saveRoute), and the
Routing page read them by the day it picked, so a reader in another zone was
given this computer's day: a route at 23:50 here (Honolulu's 00:50 the next
morning there) was under the day before, and the day it belongs to showed
none of it.

The files keep their names. What changed is the read side: History takes the
reader's zone (usage.Zone, which the page now names in ?tz=, as the Usage
page does), works out the one or two of this computer's days that day spans
(historyDaysFor), reads those files, and keeps a route only if it is the
reader's day — a route whose time couldn't be read stays the file's day's.
HistoryRoute, the single-route replay, searches the reader's day and its
neighbours in the reader's zone. internal/gui/trace.go passes the zone from
?tz= to both endpoints, internal/gui/context.go reads its days in it, and
plugins/routing/client.js adds ?tz= to the two calls it makes (the browser's
zone, browserZone).

Verified: red without the read side's zone — with History's file days left
as the server's (historyDaysFor returning the day asked, no per-route
check) `go test -tags nogui ./internal/gateway/ -count=1 -run
TestHistoryReadsTheReadersDay` failed: "2026-09-30 there: 1 routes, want
none: 23:50 here is 2026-10-01 there" and "2026-10-01 there: 0 routes, want
route 7". Green with it, and the same route is found by HistoryRoute under
both days. go test ./internal/gateway -run 'TestHistory' and go test
./internal/gui ok; gofmt -l silent, go vet -tags nogui ./internal/gateway/
./internal/gui/ silent, go build -tags nogui ./... ok.

Known limit, in History's comment as a shortcut: the days listed and their
numbers are still the files' days — counting a day in the reader's zone
means reading each route's time, which the tally doesn't (a 350 MB day,
#3383) — so a reader far away may see a day's number include routes the next
day there shows. The page's JS tests were not run: playwright isn't
installed in this worktree, so `node --test` fails on the require.
